<!--Engine Documentation — Introduction
  FQN: org.blockml.bml.engine.blockrender.documentation.Introduction-->
<edoc:Introduction xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:edoc="org.blockml.bml.engine.blockrender.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    Purpose of the block render engine
  </is>
  <documentation>
    CompilerEngine is the generic term for the processing infrastructure that
    BlockProcessors use. BOM Core defines that contract as the TypeScript
    interface RenderEngine. This package is the V1 implementation.

    It was designed around MakerUniverse use cases — physical blocks, electronics,
    services — and was first named maker-engine / MakerEngine / @blockml/engine-maker.
    The behaviour is unchanged. The name is block-render-engine because the job
    is to host several render strategies in one project, not to be a MachineBlocks
    product.

    V1 covers the strategies those use cases need:

    - engine.compile — deterministic code transformation
    - engine.renderTemplate / engine.renderTemplates — template file plus substitution
    - engine.copyTemplate — file copy, no transformation
    - engine.callLLM — LLM generation, specified, not implemented in V1

    New engines can be published as other packages that implement the same
    interface. Examples of that shape (not this package): an XSLT engine, a Rust
    codegen engine. There is no fixed set of engines.

    The engine is browser and Node compatible. No Node-only APIs.

    BlockProcessors and RenderTarget dispatch live with each target. This library
    specifies only what those processors may call, and the context/output shapes
    the call expects. Dispatch order (exact FQN, then baseType chain, then
    delegates, else BML_NO_TARGET) is target behaviour; a delegate-only target
    may omit the engine entirely.

  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="edoc:Naming" relation="ReadNext" />
  </references>
</edoc:Introduction>
